          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When someone says they have heart failure, does that mean the heart has stopped working?",
          "answer": "No, it does not mean the heart has stopped. The \"Heart failure\" overview in the General clinical reference (educational summary) says the name is misleading. Heart failure means the heart cannot fill with or pump out blood well enough to meet the body's needs. It is a long-term condition that is usually managed, often for many years.\n\nThe same overview explains that doctors sort heart failure by ejection fraction, which is the share of blood the heart's main pumping chamber pushes out with each beat. About half of cases have a preserved ejection fraction. The source describes that as a problem of stiffness and poor filling, not weak pumping, and not a milder form.\n\nThe documents I searched did not cover symptoms, causes, or treatment of heart failure in any detail. If someone you know has been told they have it, a clinician can explain what their own heart function tests show and what it means for them.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
